8. CRITICAL TOKEN ECONOMICS REPAIR: REMOVE USER-CONTROLLED DRAIN OVERRIDES

Inspect

crates/vnetwork-core/src/token.rs
crates/vnetwork-core/src/drain.rs
crates/vnetwork-types/
crates/vnetwork-core/src/language/
crates/vnetwork-api/

Inspect the current implementation of:

override_drain_bps: Option<u16>

and any equivalent request parameter.

An ordinary sender must not be allowed to override the protocol drain policy.

A sender must not be able to reduce a required drain simply by submitting a lower rate or zero.

Required correction

Remove user-controlled drain overrides from ordinary public transfer requests.

Calculate the applicable drain from an approved, deterministic, versioned protocol policy.

The policy must evaluate the actual transaction scope:

SAME WALLET
    Very low scope cost
DIFFERENT WALLETS / SAME REGION
    Higher scope cost
DIFFERENT REGIONS / SAME SPACE
    Very high scope cost
DIFFERENT SPACES
    Extremely high scope cost

Calculate scope from verified source and destination entity relationships.

Do not trust a client-submitted scope label.

Policy requirements

The drain policy must have:

* Version.
* Effective protocol context.
* Scope classification.
* Integer arithmetic.
* Defined precision.
* Defined rounding.
* Minimum and maximum.
* Supply-accounting behaviour.
* Authorization requirements.
* Event history.
* Replay support.
* Reconstruction support.
* Consensus commitment where required.

Use the approved policy configuration.

Do not invent final drain percentages or economic rates.

If a required policy value has not been approved, represent it as explicitly unconfigured and prevent affected operations from bypassing it through an implicit zero or arbitrary default.

Do not silently apply a new economic rule merely to make the tests pass.

Token-only invariant

Drains must affect only the transferable token dimension.

They must never change:

* authratio(au);
* energy.

Destination authorization

Inspect the source and destination Wallet permission evaluation.

The source needs appropriate send authority.

The destination must satisfy its applicable receive policy.

Do not treat the recipient’s lack of a signed transaction as automatic rejection if the approved receiving model allows open receiving.

Implement the proper Wallet receive policy explicitly, supporting the permissions and restrictions defined by the protocol.

A frozen, restricted or unauthorized receiving target must be handled according to its policy.

A sender must never be able to bypass destination restrictions.

Mandatory tests

Test all four transaction scopes.

For each scope, test:

* Correct policy selection.
* Sender cannot override the policy.
* Zero override is rejected.
* Negative or excessive values are rejected.
* Unconfigured policy fails safely.
* Deterministic calculation.
* Integer rounding.
* Supply consistency.
* Correct Token-only dimension changes.
* Unauthorized source rejection.
* Destination receiving restrictions.

